sections.body:
An interface type is a set of method signatures -- nothing more:

```go
type Shape interface {
	Area() float64
	Perimeter() float64
}
```

Any type that has methods matching *every* signature `Shape` declares automatically satisfies it, the instant that's true -- there is no `type Circle implements Shape` declaration anywhere in Go, because the language has no `implements` keyword at all:

```go
type Circle struct{ Radius float64 }

func (c Circle) Area() float64      { return math.Pi * c.Radius * c.Radius }
func (c Circle) Perimeter() float64 { return 2 * math.Pi * c.Radius }

type Rectangle struct{ Width, Height float64 }

func (r Rectangle) Area() float64      { return r.Width * r.Height }
func (r Rectangle) Perimeter() float64 { return 2 * (r.Width + r.Height) }

func describe(s Shape) {
	fmt.Printf("area=%.2f perimeter=%.2f\n", s.Area(), s.Perimeter())
}

describe(Circle{Radius: 2})       // works -- Circle has both methods
describe(Rectangle{Width: 3, Height: 4})   // works -- so does Rectangle
```

This is worth being precise about, because it's easy to overstate: the *type-checking itself* is exactly as static and strict as ever -- `describe(42)` still fails to compile, with an error naming exactly which method `int` is missing. What's implicit is only the *declaration* of the relationship: nothing anywhere says 'Circle satisfies Shape' in so many words; the compiler simply checks, at each call site where an interface is required, whether the concrete value provided has the right methods, and either accepts it or reports precisely what's missing.

Because satisfaction is purely structural -- based on the shape of a type's method set, not on any declared ancestry -- an interface can be defined *after* a concrete type already exists, in a completely different package that the original type's author never anticipated, without touching that type's source at all. Contrast this with a language requiring an explicit `implements` clause: the class's own author has to know about, and declare, every interface it implements, at the point the class is written.

This decoupling has a very practical consequence for you as a caller of someone else's code: you can define a small interface, entirely on your own side, capturing *exactly* the subset of methods your code actually needs from some large type -- including a standard-library type, or a third-party dependency you have no ability to modify -- and use that interface to accept substitutes (real implementations, or trivial fakes for testing) without the original type's package ever needing to know your interface exists.

```go
// You define this, in your own package -- *log.Logger never mentions it.
type Logger interface {
	Printf(format string, args ...any)
}

func process(logger Logger, item string) {
	logger.Printf("processing %s", item)
}

// A real *log.Logger satisfies your interface automatically -- it happens
// to have a matching Printf method, with no coordination required.
process(log.New(os.Stdout, "", 0), "order-42")
```

In a test, you can pass in any small struct with a matching `Printf` method instead -- one that simply records calls in a slice for later inspection -- and `process` neither knows nor cares that it isn't talking to a real `*log.Logger`.

This gotcha shows up in real code almost exclusively through one specific shape: a function that declares a named pointer variable of some concrete error-implementing type, only conditionally assigns it, and then returns that variable through an `error`-typed return slot regardless of whether it was ever actually set:

```go
func validate(input string) error {
	var problem *MyError
	if len(input) > 100 {
		problem = &MyError{msg: "input too long"}
	}
	return problem   // BUG: always returns a non-nil error interface, even when problem is still nil
}

err := validate("short input")
if err != nil {
	fmt.Println("validation failed:", err)   // runs even though input was actually fine!
}
```

Even when `input` is short and `problem` is never assigned, `return problem` still returns a non-nil `error`-typed value, for exactly the reason covered above: the interface's type half gets populated with `*MyError` the instant a `*MyError`-typed value -- even a nil one -- flows into an `error`-typed return slot. The caller's `err != nil` check is therefore *always* true, regardless of whether validation actually failed. The fix is to be deliberate about the 'no error' case: `return nil` explicitly whenever there is genuinely no failure, rather than returning a possibly-nil concrete pointer variable through an interface-typed slot -- for example, restructuring `validate` to `if len(input) > 100 { return &MyError{...} }; return nil`. The general rule worth internalizing: think twice before returning a variable of a concrete pointer type through an interface-typed return value when that variable might still be nil at the point of return.

