sections.body:
A struct field is normally declared with a name and a type: `Engine Engine`. **Embedding** is what happens when you declare a field using only its type, with no separate field name -- the type name itself becomes the field's (implicit) name:

```go
type Engine struct{ Horsepower int }
func (e Engine) Start() string { return "vroom" }

type CarWithNamedField struct {
	Engine Engine   // a normal, named field
}

type CarWithEmbedding struct {
	Engine          // embedded -- no separate field name, just the type
}

named := CarWithNamedField{Engine: Engine{Horsepower: 300}}
fmt.Println(named.Engine.Start())   // must go through .Engine explicitly

embedded := CarWithEmbedding{Engine: Engine{Horsepower: 300}}
fmt.Println(embedded.Start())        // promoted -- callable directly on CarWithEmbedding
fmt.Println(embedded.Engine.Start()) // still also reachable explicitly, exactly like a named field
```

The embedded field is still genuinely there, still addressable via its type name (`embedded.Engine`) exactly like any named field would be -- embedding doesn't remove it, it *additionally* promotes its methods (and fields) up to the outer type. `embedded.Start()` is not some new, separate method on `CarWithEmbedding` -- it's syntactic sugar the compiler generates, effectively rewriting the call to `embedded.Engine.Start()` for you. There's no dynamic dispatch or virtual-table indirection involved beyond what an ordinary method call already has; it's a compile-time convenience, not a runtime mechanism.

Both the *fields* and the *methods* of an embedded type are promoted, not just methods:

```go
type Engine struct{ Horsepower int }
func (e Engine) Start() string { return "vroom" }

type Car struct {
	Engine
	Brand string
}

c := Car{Engine: Engine{Horsepower: 300}, Brand: "Zephyr"}
fmt.Println(c.Horsepower)   // 300 -- Engine's own field, promoted directly onto Car
fmt.Println(c.Start())         // "vroom" -- Engine's own method, promoted directly onto Car
```

Promotion is also **recursive**: if `A` embeds `B`, and `B` itself embeds `C`, then `A` gets `C`'s promoted fields and methods too, chained through `B`, as long as nothing along the way introduces a naming conflict (covered next). This lets you build up a struct from several layers of small, independently-defined pieces, each contributing its own promoted surface to whatever embeds it, without writing any forwarding code by hand at any layer.

If the outer struct defines its own method or field with the same name as something promoted from an embedded type, the outer one **shadows** it: unqualified access (`c.Start()`) resolves to the outer type's own version, and the embedded version remains reachable only by explicitly qualifying it with the embedded type's name (`c.Engine.Start()`).

```go
type Car struct {
	Engine
	Brand string
}

func (c Car) Start() string {
	result := c.Engine.Start()   // explicitly reaching the embedded method
	return c.Brand + " says: " + result
}

c := Car{Engine: Engine{Horsepower: 300}, Brand: "Zephyr"}
fmt.Println(c.Start())          // "Zephyr says: vroom"

var e Engine = c.Engine
fmt.Println(e.Start())          // "vroom" -- Engine's own Start, with no knowledge of Car at all
```

This last line is the single most important thing to understand about why embedding is not inheritance: `e`, a plain `Engine` value copied out of `c.Engine`, calls `Engine`'s own `Start()` and knows nothing whatsoever about `Car`'s override -- there is no mechanism by which an `Engine`-typed value or variable could ever be redirected to `Car`'s version. In a language with real inheritance and virtual dispatch, a base-class reference that actually points at a derived-class object *would* invoke the derived class's override. Go's embedding gives you promotion and shadowing purely at the outer type's own call sites -- it does not give any value typed as the embedded type a way to reach 'upward' toward whatever might be embedding it, because, mechanically, there is no such relationship recorded anywhere at all.

You can embed an interface type, not just a concrete struct type, inside a struct -- and the same promotion rules apply: any method the interface declares becomes callable directly on the outer struct, forwarded to whatever concrete value the embedded interface field currently holds.

```go
type PartialWriter struct {
	io.Writer   // embedded interface -- zero value is nil until explicitly set
}

func (p PartialWriter) Write(data []byte) (int, error) {
	fmt.Println("intercepted:", string(data))
	return p.Writer.Write(data)   // forwards to whatever was actually assigned
}
```

A common, deliberate use of this pattern is building a lightweight test double or wrapper that only needs to implement *some* of a large interface's methods -- embedding the interface lets the outer struct satisfy that interface's full method set for compile-time purposes, while the struct's own explicitly-defined methods override just the ones it actually cares about, and any method it doesn't override falls through to the embedded interface value. This is exactly where the real risk lives: the embedded interface's **zero value is nil**, just like any interface variable's zero value -- if it's never explicitly assigned a real implementation, and some code calls a promoted method that the outer struct never overrode, that call forwards straight into a nil interface value, which panics at runtime with a nil pointer dereference, precisely because there's no actual concrete implementation behind it to forward the call to.

