sections.body:
Composition lets you assemble a type from a handful of small, independently useful, independently swappable pieces, rather than inheriting downward through a fixed chain of ancestors:

```go
type Logger interface{ Log(msg string) }
type Cache interface{ Get(key string) (string, bool) }

type Server struct {
	Logger   // one small, swappable piece
	Cache    // another
	Port int
}
```

Compare this to a hypothetical `BaseServer -> HTTPServer -> AuthenticatedServer -> LoggingAuthenticatedServer` inheritance chain, where each layer bakes in one more piece of fixed behavior. With composition, you can construct a `Server` with any `Logger` implementation and any `Cache` implementation you like -- swap in a no-op logger for tests, or a Redis-backed cache in production, and a completely different combination somewhere else -- all without touching `Server`'s own definition at all, as long as whatever you plug in satisfies the small interface in that slot. A rigid, multi-level inheritance chain, by contrast, locks in each layer's behavior at a fixed position; swapping behavior somewhere in the *middle* of that chain typically means either editing an existing class that other subclasses also depend on, or threading in a brand new subclass wired in at precisely the right point -- a change with a much larger, harder-to-predict blast radius than swapping one small interface implementation.

examples.code:
package main

import "fmt"

// Reuse: any struct that embeds Timestamped gets CreatedAt and Age() for free.
type Timestamped struct{ CreatedAt int }
func (t Timestamped) Age(now int) int { return now - t.CreatedAt }

// Polymorphism: any type with a Notify method satisfies this, regardless of ancestry.
type Notifier interface{ Notify(message string) }

type EmailAlert struct {
	Timestamped
	To string
}
func (e EmailAlert) Notify(message string) { fmt.Println("email to", e.To+":", message) }

type SMSAlert struct {
	Timestamped
	Phone string
}
func (s SMSAlert) Notify(message string) { fmt.Println("sms to", s.Phone+":", message) }

func broadcast(notifiers []Notifier, message string) {
	for _, n := range notifiers {
		n.Notify(message)
	}
}

func main() {
	alerts := []Notifier{
		EmailAlert{Timestamped: Timestamped{CreatedAt: 10}, To: "amy@example.com"},
		SMSAlert{Timestamped: Timestamped{CreatedAt: 12}, Phone: "555-0100"},
	}
	broadcast(alerts, "system maintenance at midnight")

package main

import "fmt"

type Cache interface {
	Get(key string) (string, bool)
}

type memoryCache struct{ data map[string]string }
func (m *memoryCache) Get(key string) (string, bool) { v, ok := m.data[key]; return v, ok }

type alwaysMissCache struct{}
func (alwaysMissCache) Get(key string) (string, bool) { return "", false }

type Service struct {
	Cache   // embedded interface -- whichever implementation is supplied, promoted directly
	Name string
}

func main() {
	real := Service{Cache: &memoryCache{data: map[string]string{"a": "1"}}, Name: "prod"}
	v, ok := real.Get("a")
	fmt.Println(v, ok)   // 1 true

	test := Service{Cache: alwaysMissCache{}, Name: "test"}
	v, ok = test.Get("a")
	fmt.Println(v, ok)   // '' false

// Anti-pattern: mimicking a class hierarchy through nested embedding.
type Base struct{ ID int }
type Named struct { Base; Name string }
type Timestamped struct { Named; CreatedAt int }
type FullRecord struct { Timestamped; Notes string }
// FullRecord now promotes through three layers -- ID, Name, CreatedAt, and Notes --
// but each layer is rigidly, sequentially dependent on the one directly beneath it.

// Flatter alternative: one level, composed of a few independent, named pieces.
type Record struct {
	ID        int
	Name      string
	CreatedAt int
	Notes     string
}

